1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,572
Coach: Once you understand a contribution, you can decide what it needs from you.

2
00:00:05,572 --> 00:00:07,714
Coach: Sometimes it needs a question.

3
00:00:07,714 --> 00:00:10,286
Coach: Sometimes a brief confirmation is enough.

4
00:00:10,286 --> 00:00:15,000
Coach: In this section, we will look at agreement and useful additions,

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,429
Coach: without turning every reply into a long speech.

6
00:00:18,779 --> 00:00:20,922
Coach: Return to the planning group.

7
00:00:20,922 --> 00:00:26,493
Coach: They have thirty minutes for a beginner paper-folding workshop, with one facilitator.

8
00:00:26,493 --> 00:00:31,207
Coach: They want participants to have an opportunity to try the activity

9
00:00:31,207 --> 00:00:32,493
Coach: and ask questions.

10
00:00:32,493 --> 00:00:35,493
Coach: The group has not chosen a plan.

11
00:00:35,843 --> 00:00:40,986
Noah: I would keep the demonstration brief, so people have time to try.

12
00:00:40,986 --> 00:00:46,557
Noah: I am worried that someone who misses a step could get left behind.

13
00:00:46,907 --> 00:00:50,336
Coach: Imagine that you support leaving time for practice.

14
00:00:50,336 --> 00:00:52,479
Coach: A simple yes communicates support.

15
00:00:52,479 --> 00:00:57,622
Coach: But if the group still needs to work out what you support,

16
00:00:57,622 --> 00:00:59,336
Coach: name the specific point.

17
00:00:59,336 --> 00:01:02,336
Coach: That makes your agreement easier to use.

18
00:01:02,686 --> 00:01:07,400
You: I agree that people need time to try the activity themselves.

19
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:11,179
Coach: This supports the reason for a shorter demonstration.

20
00:01:11,179 --> 00:01:15,036
Coach: It does not mean that you accept every detail,

21
00:01:15,036 --> 00:01:18,893
Coach: or that the concern about missed steps has disappeared.

22
00:01:18,893 --> 00:01:24,036
Coach: Keep the part you support separate from the part that remains open.

23
00:01:30,386 --> 00:01:33,386
Coach: Now decide whether another detail would help.

24
00:01:33,386 --> 00:01:39,386
Coach: An addition is relevant when it changes something the group still needs to decide.

25
00:01:39,386 --> 00:01:41,529
Coach: Listen to this possible continuation.

26
00:01:41,879 --> 00:01:45,736
You: We could keep a step card visible during practice,

27
00:01:45,736 --> 00:01:48,736
You: so people can check where they are.

28
00:01:49,086 --> 00:01:50,372
Maya: That might help.

29
00:01:50,372 --> 00:01:55,515
Maya: Would the card be clear enough for someone who is completely new?

30
00:01:55,865 --> 00:01:58,436
Coach: The addition addresses a specific concern,

31
00:01:58,436 --> 00:02:02,722
Coach: and Maya now has a more precise question to examine.

32
00:02:02,722 --> 00:02:04,865
Coach: That is a useful connection.

33
00:02:04,865 --> 00:02:08,722
Coach: It is not evidence that the card will work,

34
00:02:08,722 --> 00:02:11,722
Coach: or that every beginner would understand it.

35
00:02:12,072 --> 00:02:15,929
Coach: A different sentence might still be about the workshop

36
00:02:15,929 --> 00:02:18,072
Coach: without helping with this concern.

37
00:02:18,072 --> 00:02:22,786
Coach: For example, suggesting a bright poster is on the same topic,

38
00:02:22,786 --> 00:02:27,929
Coach: but it does not explain how someone would recover a missed step.

39
00:02:27,929 --> 00:02:31,786
Coach: It could matter later, when publicity is the question.

40
00:02:32,136 --> 00:02:37,279
Coach: Do not add a new idea simply to make the reply longer.

41
00:02:37,279 --> 00:02:42,851
Coach: If the group has already checked a detail and is confirming the arrangement,

42
00:02:42,851 --> 00:02:46,708
Coach: a clear yes may be all that is needed.

43
00:02:46,708 --> 00:02:50,565
Coach: The value of your response depends on the moment.

44
00:02:50,915 --> 00:02:53,058
Coach: Before adding something, ask yourself:

45
00:02:53,058 --> 00:02:59,058
Coach: which point am I responding to, and what work will the extra detail do?

46
00:02:59,058 --> 00:03:04,200
Coach: You might clarify a method, identify a limit, or ask a question.

47
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:07,629
Coach: You do not have to do all three.

48
00:03:07,979 --> 00:03:10,979
Coach: Keep Noah's reason and concern on screen.

49
00:03:10,979 --> 00:03:17,408
Coach: Form a short reply that supports the time for practice and adds one relevant detail,

50
00:03:17,408 --> 00:03:19,979
Coach: if you think one is needed.

51
00:03:19,979 --> 00:03:23,408
Coach: Think it through privately, or say it aloud.

52
00:03:23,408 --> 00:03:25,979
Coach: Pause if you want more time.

53
00:03:38,329 --> 00:03:41,758
You: I agree that people need time to try.

54
00:03:41,758 --> 00:03:47,758
You: Could we check whether a simple step card would help someone follow the activity?

55
00:03:48,108 --> 00:03:52,822
Coach: This model supports a particular reason and suggests something to examine.

56
00:03:52,822 --> 00:03:57,537
Coach: You may have chosen another relevant addition, or a shorter response.

57
00:03:57,537 --> 00:04:03,108
Coach: Compare the connection to Noah's point, not whether you used the same words.

58
00:04:03,458 --> 00:04:07,744
Coach: Keep agreement precise, and give an addition a clear purpose.

59
00:04:07,744 --> 00:04:14,601
Coach: Next we will look at a concern that a relevant suggestion still does not fully answer.
